You’re in a situation where tone and framing matter more than technical detail.

Right now your mindset is a little “this happens all the time / others do worse.”
Do not lead with that. That argument will hurt you in a privacy/compliance conversation.

Instead, your approach needs to be:

🎯 Your Game Plan
1. Stay in facts + process, not emotion

Do not argue.
Do not compare to other issues.
Do not say “this happens all the time.”

Compliance cares about:

Was there a process
Was it followed
Was there risk to PHI / patient care
2. Frame your action as a safety control

This is your strongest position:

You did not “block care” — you prevented unvalidated data from entering PACS

That aligns with:

Data integrity
Patient safety
Privacy (wrong routing = potential exposure)
3. Be ready to explain your workflow simply

If they ask what happened, your explanation should sound like this:

“When an AE Title does not match an approved configuration, the connection is placed on hold. This prevents unknown or misconfigured systems from sending images into PACS. Once the correct AE Title is confirmed, I validate and allow routing.”

That’s it. Clean. Controlled. No rambling.

4. Be clear about your dependency (this protects you)

You should clearly state:

“I rely on the Modality Interface request and BioMed/vendor coordination to know what configuration is expected.”

That shows:

You are not acting independently
You are part of a controlled process chain
5. If they bring up patient impact

Acknowledge—but don’t accept fault:

“I understand there was a delay, and that’s not ideal. At the same time, allowing an unrecognized AE Title through without validation could introduce incorrect or misrouted studies, which also presents patient safety and data integrity risks.”

This is critical:

You’re not dismissing impact
You’re showing why your action was necessary
6. If they ask about manual intervention

You mentioned you manually push images through sometimes — be careful here.

If asked:

“Once the correct configuration is confirmed, I can manually validate and allow routing. However, I do not bypass validation for unknown AE Titles.”

Avoid saying:

“I usually just push them through”
“I fix it manually all the time”

That can be twisted into:

“Why didn’t you just do that here?”

🚫 What NOT to say (important)

Do NOT say:

“Images fail all the time”
“This happens everywhere”
“They didn’t tell me so it’s not my fault”
“I always fix these manually”

Even if true — it weakens your position.

